<p>How many publications do early career job applicants in academic philosophy have? How many courses have they taught? How have the answers to these questions changed over the past decade?<span id="more-57078"></span></p>
<p>These are some of the questions addressed by <a href="https://travislacroix.github.io/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Travis LaCroix</a> in the following guest post. Dr. LaCroix is assistant professor of philosophy at Durham University and co-director of <a href="https://www.placementdata.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Academic Philosophy Data Analysis</a> (APDA).</p>
<p>The post is based on data gathered in the <a href="https://philarchive.org/rec/JENMRF-3?ref=apda.ghost.io" target="_blank" rel="noopener">2025 APDA survey</a>.</p>
<p>(A version of this post also appears at the <a href="https://apda.ghost.io/2025-survey-results-publications-and-teaching-in-academia-part-i-professional-norms-and-academic-outcomes/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">APDA blog</a>.)</p>
